UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 37, Lesson 2: Fitness challenge circuits', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A fitness challenge circuit builds strength, stamina and flexibility using stations such as step-ups, wall push-ups, chair squats, skipping and stretching. Warm up first, work at your own pace for 30 to 40 seconds per station, rest briefly and cool down with gentle stretches. Challenge yourself, not others, by comparing with your own earlier results. Stop if you feel pain or dizziness and keep water close.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA circuit has no warm-up and no water break. Write one change for each and say why.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd 2 minutes of marching and arm circles first to prepare the muscles, and a water break after each round to replace fluid. Warm-ups help prevent injury and water breaks keep the body safe.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving you should push through pain. Stop if anything hurts or you feel unwell; feeling puffed from effort is different from pain.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDesign a five-station fitness circuit using household items, with a warm-up, a cool-down, timings and easier and harder choices. Do it with family, record your counts at each station, repeat in a week and compare with your own results. Stop if you feel unwell.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A fitness challenge circuit builds strength, stamina and flexibility using stations such as step-ups, wall push-ups, chair squats, skipping and stretching. Warm up first, work at your own pace for 30 to 40 seconds per station, rest briefly and cool down with gentle stretches. Challenge yourself, not others, by comparing with your own earlier results. Stop if you feel pain or dizziness and keep water close.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Design a five-station fitness circuit using household items, with a warm-up, a cool-down, timings and easier and harder choices. Do it with family, record your counts at each station, repeat in a week and compare with yo Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a fitness circuit on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing you should push through pain.. Correction: Add 2 minutes of marching and arm circles first to prepare the muscles, and a water break after each round to replace. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing you should push through pain. Stop if anything hurts or you feel unwell; feeling puffed from effort is different from pain. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a fitness circuit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a fitness circuit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a fitness circuit with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a fitness circuit. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a fitness circuit. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Add 2 minutes of marching and arm circles first to prepare the muscles, and a water break after each round to replace","Believing you should push through pain"],"question":"A circuit has no warm-up and no water break. Write one change for each and say why. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a fitness circuit; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing you should push through pain","A fitness challenge circuit builds strength, stamina and flexibility using stations such as step-ups, wall push-ups","Add 2 minutes of marching and arm circles first to prepare the muscles, and a water break after each round to replace","Careful use of warm-up and cool-down and explain why it with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a fitness circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named warm-up and cool-down and explain why it, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing you should push through pain","Name warm-up and cool-down and explain why it only, without linking it to a fitness circuit","Apply a fitness circuit to new material and name the warm-up and cool-down and explain why it used"],"question":"Which response best applies a fitness circuit with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on a fitness circuit. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing you should push through pain","Add 2 minutes of marching and arm circles first to prepare the muscles, and a water break after each round to replace","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for a fitness circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing you should push through pain","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with a fitness circuit","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a fitness circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A fitness challenge circuit builds strength, stamina and flexibility) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example circuit. 1 Step-ups on a low step. 2 Wall push-ups. 3 Chair squats (sit down to a chair and stand up). 4","Believing you should push through pain","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a fitness circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for a fitness circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing you should push through pain","Name warm-up and cool-down and explain why it only, with no linked example","Design a five-station fitness circuit using household items, with a warm-up, a cool-down, timings and easier and harder"],"question":"Which independent response best applies a fitness circuit to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a fitness circuit with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a fitness circuit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a fitness circuit on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a fitness circuit. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a fitness circuit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing you should push through pain.. Correction: Add 2 minutes of marching and arm circles first to prepare the muscles, and a water break after each round to replace. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w37-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a fitness circuit.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a fitness circuit with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify warm-up and cool-down and explain why it matters.","I can practise your own results independently on a new prompt.","I can notice stopping if unwell needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A fitness challenge circuit builds strength, stamina and flexibility using stations such as step-ups, wall push-ups, chair squats, skipping and stretching.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","A fitness challenge circuit builds strength, stamina and flexibility using stations such as step-ups, wall push-ups"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a fitness circuit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample circuit. 1 Step-ups on a low step. 2 Wall push-ups. 3 Chair squats (sit down to a chair and stand up). 4 Skipping. 5 Balance on one foot. 30 seconds each, 15 seconds rest. Warm-up: march on the spot for 2 minutes. Cool-down: gentle arm and leg stretches. Sara records 12 chair squats, then 15 the next week.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA circuit has no warm-up and no water break. Write one change for each and say why.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd 2 minutes of marching and arm circles first to prepare the muscles, and a water break after each round to replace fluid. Warm-ups help prevent injury and water breaks keep the body safe.","explain":"A fitness challenge circuit builds strength, stamina and flexibility using stations such as step-ups, wall push-ups, chair squats, skipping and stretching. Warm up first, work at your own pace for 30 to 40 seconds per station, rest briefly and cool down with gentle stretches. Challenge yourself, not others, by comparing with your own earlier results. Stop if you feel pain or dizziness and keep water close.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing you should push through pain. Stop if anything hurts or you feel unwell; feeling puffed from effort is different from pain. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A fitness circuit.","caption":"Rotate and rest.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stations</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">step-ups, squats, skipping</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">30 seconds, 15 rest</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example circuit. 1 Step-ups on a low step. 2 Wall push-ups. 3 Chair squats (sit down to a chair and stand up). 4 Skippin Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example circuit. 1 Step-ups on a low step. 2 Wall push-ups. 3 Chair squats (sit down to a chair and stand up). 4 Skippin Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a fitness circuit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample circuit. 1 Step-ups on a low step. 2 Wall push-ups. 3 Chair squats (sit down to a chair and stand up). 4 Skipping. 5 Balance on one foot. 30 seconds each, 15 seconds rest. Warm-up: march on the spot for 2 minutes. Cool-down: gentle arm and leg stretches. Sara records 12 chair squats, then 15 the next week. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Warm-up and cool-down.","caption":"Start and end gently.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Warm-up</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">march, arm circles</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cool-down</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">gentle stretches</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a fitness circuit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving you should push through pain. Stop if anything hurts or you feel unwell; feeling puffed from effort is different from pain. Here is a more helpful example: Example circuit. 1 Step-ups on a low step. 2 Wall push-ups. 3 Chair squats (sit down to a chair and stand up). 4 Skipping. 5 Balance on one foot. 30 seconds each, 15 seconds rest. Warm-up: march on the spot for 2 minutes. Cool-down: gentle arm and leg stretches. Sara records 12 chair squats, then 15 the next week.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd 2 minutes of marching and arm circles first to prepare the muscles, and a water break after each round to replace fluid. Warm-ups help prevent injury and water breaks keep the body safe.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing you should push through pain. Stop if anything hurts or you feel unwell; feeling puffed from effort is different from pain. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a fitness circuit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA fitness challenge circuit builds strength, stamina and flexibility using stations such as step-ups, wall push-ups, chair squats, skipping and stretching. Warm up first, work at your own pace for 30 to 40 seconds per station, rest briefly and cool down with gentle stretches. Challenge yourself, not others, by comparing with your own earlier results. Stop if you feel pain or dizziness and keep water close. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 37, Lesson 2: Fitness challenge circuits","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Healthy habits","week":37,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample circuit. 1 Step-ups on a low step. 2 Wall push-ups. 3 Chair squats (sit down to a chair and stand up). 4 Skipping. 5 Balance on one foot. 30 seconds each, 15 seconds rest. Warm-up: march on the spot for 2 minutes. Cool-down: gentle arm and leg stretches. Sara records 12 chair squats, then 15 the next week.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A fitness circuit.","caption":"Rotate and rest.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stations</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">step-ups, squats, skipping</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">30 seconds, 15 rest</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Warm-up and cool-down.","caption":"Start and end gently.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Warm-up</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">march, arm circles</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cool-down</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">gentle stretches</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w37-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
